Market Challenges
Motion artefacts, changing lighting conditions, and interference from caregivers or bedside equipment can affect measurement accuracy. Camera-based systems require suitable visibility, while radar sensors must distinguish neonatal breathing movements from other activity around the infant.

Clinical validation is another major challenge. Monitoring technologies must demonstrate reliable performance across different gestational ages, patient conditions, and care environments. Integration with existing hospital monitors, alarm systems, and patient records also requires careful technical planning.

Market Restraints
Regulatory approval and clinical performance validation remain important restraints. Hospitals require evidence that new systems can deliver dependable measurements and operate safely alongside established monitoring equipment.

Budget limitations, integration requirements, and uncertainty around reimbursement can further slow purchasing decisions. Consumer breathing and movement monitors also create market confusion when their features are compared with regulated medical monitoring systems despite differences in intended use.

